1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:01,900
March eighth, twenty twenty-three.

2
00:00:01,900 --> 00:00:10,129
A streaming service uploads three documentary episodes about a vanished airliner, timed to the ninth anniversary of the day it disappeared.

3
00:00:11,266 --> 00:00:16,961
Inside those episodes, one man argues two completely different explanations for the same disappearance.

4
00:00:16,961 --> 00:00:22,655
In the first hour, the pilot did it. In the second, Russian hijackers did.

5
00:00:23,800 --> 00:00:25,524
Neither argument has held up.

6
00:00:26,666 --> 00:00:31,404
Within days, the investigators who spent over a decade on this case are furious.

7
00:00:31,404 --> 00:00:41,895
And the director herself later admits, on the record, that she believes the plane is somewhere else entirely from what her own series just spent two and a half hours dramatizing.

8
00:00:43,033 --> 00:00:50,269
Millions of people now believe things about M-H three seven zero that the people who actually spent years searching for it say are simply wrong.

9
00:00:51,400 --> 00:00:53,221
This is what they got wrong.

10
00:00:53,221 --> 00:01:02,633
And this is what the actual flight recorder math says happened, in its final seconds — math neither of our first two M-H three seven zero videos has fully shown you.

11
00:01:03,766 --> 00:01:05,804
You're watching Black Box Breakdown.

12
00:01:06,933 --> 00:01:10,721
M-H three seven zero. Fact-checking the Netflix documentary.

13
00:01:11,866 --> 00:01:14,217
Chapter one. The documentary.

14
00:01:15,366 --> 00:01:17,004
March eighth, twenty twenty-three.

15
00:01:17,004 --> 00:01:26,834
Nine years to the day after the disappearance, a three-part documentary series drops, all at once: M-H three seven zero, The Plane That Disappeared.

16
00:01:27,966 --> 00:01:32,371
Each of the three episodes centers on one theory, and one on-camera champion.

17
00:01:32,371 --> 00:01:37,454
Episode one: the pilot did it. Episode two: Russian hijackers did. Episode three: the U.S.

18
00:01:37,454 --> 00:01:40,165
military shot it down, and covered it up.

19
00:01:41,299 --> 00:01:44,157
Critics were split right down the middle.

20
00:01:44,157 --> 00:01:46,606
Professional reviewers rated it generally favorable.

21
00:01:46,606 --> 00:01:51,096
Ordinary viewers, watching those same three episodes, rated it generally unfavorable.

22
00:01:52,233 --> 00:01:58,842
David Mearns, a marine scientist who has spent years hunting shipwrecks, including this one, was blunt about it.

23
00:01:59,966 --> 00:02:07,280
The series is dominated by two people spinning, in his words, ridiculous and unsupported conspiracy theories.

24
00:02:08,400 --> 00:02:13,885
Richard Godfrey, one of the most cited independent investigators on this case, went further.

25
00:02:13,885 --> 00:02:21,722
The series gives conspiracy, fabrication, and fantasy the same weight as hard evidence, and short on hard facts and evidence.

26
00:02:22,866 --> 00:02:27,454
And here is the detail that undercuts the entire series more than any critic could.

27
00:02:27,454 --> 00:02:37,547
Its own director, Louise Malkinson, has said publicly that she believes the plane is most likely sitting in the southern Indian Ocean — the one outcome her own show barely dramatizes at all.

28
00:02:38,666 --> 00:02:41,795
To be fair, not every review was a takedown.

29
00:02:41,795 --> 00:02:49,096
The Wall Street Journal called it a series where every convincing argument is followed by an equally convincing demolition of it.

30
00:02:49,096 --> 00:02:53,268
Even harsher critics praised episode one's minute-by-minute reconstruction of the disappearance itself.

31
00:02:54,400 --> 00:02:58,914
So before we grade its theories one by one, remember what its own director already told you.

32
00:02:58,914 --> 00:03:03,961
This is a show built to dramatize three explanations that even the person who made it doesn't fully believe.

33
00:03:05,099 --> 00:03:06,980
Chapter two. The pilot.

34
00:03:08,099 --> 00:03:10,358
Episode one makes its case plainly.

35
00:03:10,358 --> 00:03:22,780
Captain Zaharie Ahmad Shah, it argues, deliberately turned the plane, depressurized the cabin to incapacitate everyone else on board, and flew it by hand, using saved waypoints, into the emptiest ocean on Earth.

36
00:03:23,900 --> 00:03:33,617
One reviewer called the episode's extended reenactment of that moment stunningly irresponsible, warning it risked, in their words, irrevocable harm to a real family.

37
00:03:34,766 --> 00:03:41,508
Here is what the official Malaysian investigation actually found, after checking his entire background.

38
00:03:41,508 --> 00:03:45,842
No psychiatric history. No financial distress. No marital trouble.

39
00:03:46,966 --> 00:03:48,757
No religious or political extremism.

40
00:03:48,757 --> 00:03:52,340
No change in his behavior that any colleague ever flagged.

41
00:03:52,340 --> 00:03:56,997
No stress detected in his own voice, on the final air traffic recordings.

42
00:03:58,133 --> 00:04:03,265
His home flight simulator did contain a saved route running into the southern Indian Ocean.

43
00:04:03,265 --> 00:04:08,739
But the official report states plainly: it could not be conclusively linked to the actual flight.

44
00:04:09,866 --> 00:04:18,304
The series also leaves out testimony describing him as lonely, and estranged — testimony the official background check simply never corroborated.

45
00:04:19,433 --> 00:04:21,423
His own sister disagrees entirely.

46
00:04:21,423 --> 00:04:27,792
Sakinah Ahmad Shah has spoken publicly more than once, including this year, calling the theory impossible.

47
00:04:28,933 --> 00:04:33,922
Calling it impossible, and describing, in her own words, a very decent family.

48
00:04:35,066 --> 00:04:39,439
So state this plainly, because it matters more than any theory in this video.

49
00:04:39,439 --> 00:04:42,874
The official investigation did not just fail to prove this theory.

50
00:04:42,874 --> 00:04:47,559
It affirmatively found nothing in the man's life that supports it. He cannot answer back.

51
00:04:47,559 --> 00:04:49,433
The evidence never convicted him either.

52
00:04:50,566 --> 00:04:52,447
Chapter three. The hijack.

53
00:04:53,566 --> 00:04:58,293
Episode two drops the pilot theory entirely, and argues something stranger.

54
00:04:58,293 --> 00:05:06,888
Three passengers, it claims, entered the aircraft's forward electronics bay mid-flight and hijacked it electronically, diverting it north toward Kazakhstan.

55
00:05:08,033 --> 00:05:14,390
The stated motive: distracting the world from Russia's annexation of Crimea, happening at almost the exact same time.

56
00:05:14,390 --> 00:05:26,397
The theory's own author, journalist Jeff Wise, admitted even in his original piece that there was, in his words, no way to know, and no plausible motive for an act with no apparent beneficiary.

57
00:05:27,533 --> 00:05:29,508
There's a timing problem too.

58
00:05:29,508 --> 00:05:33,854
By early twenty fourteen, Russia's move into Crimea was already succeeding.

59
00:05:33,854 --> 00:05:37,015
The distraction it supposedly needed didn't need creating.

60
00:05:38,133 --> 00:05:41,951
And Netflix's own on-camera interviewees rebut the theory directly.

61
00:05:41,951 --> 00:05:48,739
Fuad Sharuji, Malaysia Airlines' own former crisis director, calls a mid-flight takeover from that compartment impossible.

62
00:05:49,866 --> 00:06:01,752
Mark Dickinson, an Inmarsat vice president, also interviewed on camera, says the idea that a hijacker predicted the satellite company's own analysis method in advance doesn't seem particularly credible at all.

63
00:06:02,899 --> 00:06:04,585
Outside satellite specialists go further.

64
00:06:04,585 --> 00:06:13,348
Mike Exner states plainly the plane cannot be flown from that compartment, and that the satellite data cannot be altered from there, as the theory claims.

65
00:06:14,466 --> 00:06:19,952
And journalist William Langewiesche's own read of the physical evidence points the opposite way entirely.

66
00:06:19,952 --> 00:06:26,900
Control was seized from inside the actual cockpit, in a twenty-minute window — not remotely, from a hidden compartment.

67
00:06:28,033 --> 00:06:31,856
And here is the twist even the documentary never saw coming.

68
00:06:31,856 --> 00:06:40,546
This January, Jeff Wise — the same man who originated this theory — posted a video titled, plainly: why the cockpit theory doesn't add up.

69
00:06:41,666 --> 00:06:49,790
He is now, on the record, arguing against the very kind of deliberate-pilot scenario episode one spent an hour dramatizing.

70
00:06:50,933 --> 00:06:55,882
One man. Two theories he authored himself, for two different episodes of the same series.

71
00:06:55,882 --> 00:06:59,841
And by this year, he no longer stands fully behind either one.

72
00:07:00,966 --> 00:07:03,160
Chapter four. The intercept.

73
00:07:04,299 --> 00:07:06,224
Episode three goes furthest of all.

74
00:07:06,224 --> 00:07:18,092
It argues the aircraft carried roughly two and a half tonnes of sensitive electronic cargo bound for China, and that American surveillance aircraft intercepted and jammed it, before it was shot down over the South China Sea.

75
00:07:19,233 --> 00:07:26,689
In this version, the Indian Ocean debris was either planted, or misidentified, as part of a cover-up spanning multiple governments.

76
00:07:26,689 --> 00:07:33,026
Its lead voice is French journalist Florence de Changy, who calls the entire case aviation's biggest con.

77
00:07:34,166 --> 00:07:44,125
On camera, Ghyslain Wattrelos, who lost his wife and two children on this flight, says an intelligence contact told him American surveillance planes know full well what happened.

78
00:07:44,125 --> 00:07:48,037
That is his own account. It has never been independently confirmed.

79
00:07:49,166 --> 00:07:53,261
Richard Godfrey's rebuttal, point by point. Malaysian radar was not falsified.

80
00:07:53,261 --> 00:08:02,567
The co-pilot's own phone WAS detected, pinging a cell tower near Penang, at the exact time the series claims the plane had already been seized.

81
00:08:03,699 --> 00:08:10,387
And the recovered debris carries manufacturer part numbers and stencils that tie it directly to this exact aircraft.

82
00:08:11,533 --> 00:08:17,972
Malaysian military radar DID detect the aircraft's turn, contrary to the documentary's claim that it went unnoticed.

83
00:08:17,972 --> 00:08:25,169
It was logged as friendly, and simply not pursued — a failure of protocol, not evidence of a cover-up.

84
00:08:26,299 --> 00:08:30,884
And the mysterious cargo the series frames as something smuggled?

85
00:08:30,884 --> 00:08:35,468
A routine commercial shipment of lithium-ion batteries, for two-way radios.

86
00:08:36,600 --> 00:08:51,401
For a cover-up this size to hold, hundreds of people, across at least four different governments, would need to stay silent for over a decade — while real, physically verifiable ocean debris kept washing up on real beaches the entire time.

87
00:08:51,401 --> 00:08:54,651
The simpler explanation is almost always the true one.

88
00:08:55,800 --> 00:08:58,386
Chapter five. What Netflix got wrong.

89
00:08:59,533 --> 00:09:05,059
Step back from the individual theories, and a pattern in the documentary itself becomes the real story.

90
00:09:05,059 --> 00:09:17,087
One critic wrote that the shoot-down theory gets minutes of dramatic buildup before an expert is finally allowed to, in his words, shrug it off as more loopy than legitimate — bordering, he wrote, on the irresponsible.

91
00:09:18,233 --> 00:09:29,231
Another reviewer called it the sin of pretending every theory deserves equal time and equal weight — with Jeff Wise functioning as the series' primary guide, across two entire episodes.

92
00:09:30,366 --> 00:09:39,520
One piece of amateur satellite imagery, crowdsourced from volunteers online, is given real on-camera credibility, implying it was suppressed by governments.

93
00:09:39,520 --> 00:09:44,315
Critics who've actually studied it call it, bluntly, blurry white blobs.

94
00:09:45,433 --> 00:09:47,439
The series also underplays something real.

95
00:09:47,439 --> 00:09:53,792
Malaysian authorities sat on radar data for roughly a week, sending the initial search to the wrong ocean entirely.

96
00:09:54,933 --> 00:09:58,372
And the sharpest fact-check of all is the one we already told you.

97
00:09:58,372 --> 00:10:07,367
The documentary's own director has said, on the record, that she believes the plane is most likely in the southern Indian Ocean — the one ending her three-part series spends the least time on.

98
00:10:08,500 --> 00:10:14,658
Some of the strongest pushback came from the families themselves, not about the crash, but about this series.

99
00:10:14,658 --> 00:10:22,528
Naren Narendran, who lost his wife on this flight, said it should be marketed as fiction, calling it a lavish exercise in gaslighting.

100
00:10:23,666 --> 00:10:25,547
The backlash reached governments too.

101
00:10:25,547 --> 00:10:36,832
Vietnam pulled episode one from its own platform entirely, objecting to claims that it failed to cooperate in the search — a real, dated, institutional response to the show itself.

102
00:10:37,966 --> 00:10:41,754
To be fair to the documentary one more time.

103
00:10:41,754 --> 00:10:53,117
Even its critics praised episode one's data-driven, minute-by-minute reconstruction of the disappearance itself, calling it disturbingly realistic, right up until, in their words, it collapses into speculation.

104
00:10:54,266 --> 00:10:55,990
Chapter six. The physics.

105
00:10:57,133 --> 00:11:00,818
None of that is the most interesting fact-check in this entire case.

106
00:11:00,818 --> 00:11:09,724
The most interesting one is buried in eight seconds of automatic satellite data that neither of our previous M-H three seven zero videos has walked you through in full.

107
00:11:10,866 --> 00:11:17,238
In the final minute, the aircraft's satellite terminal made one unscheduled attempt to log back onto the network.

108
00:11:17,238 --> 00:11:21,132
Eight seconds later, it tried again, and then went silent forever.

109
00:11:22,266 --> 00:11:26,400
That is not a coincidence, and it is not a person.

110
00:11:26,400 --> 00:11:33,917
When an aircraft's engines flame out from fuel exhaustion, a backup generator automatically starts, and briefly re-powers the satellite link.

111
00:11:35,066 --> 00:11:41,832
Just long enough to force one last, involuntary attempt to reconnect, before that backup runs dry too, minutes later.

112
00:11:42,966 --> 00:11:50,517
Analysts measured the tiny frequency shift in those two signals, caused by the aircraft's own vertical motion, and calculated a descent rate.

113
00:11:50,517 --> 00:11:55,322
At the first signal: somewhere around three thousand to fifteen thousand feet per minute.

114
00:11:56,466 --> 00:12:02,735
Eight seconds later, at the second signal: somewhere between fourteen thousand and twenty-five thousand feet per minute.

115
00:12:03,866 --> 00:12:08,384
A normal passenger jet descends at around two thousand feet per minute.

116
00:12:08,384 --> 00:12:14,785
This aircraft, in its final seconds, was falling seven to thirteen times faster than that, and accelerating.

117
00:12:15,933 --> 00:12:20,030
The recovered wing flap tells the same story from a different angle.

118
00:12:20,030 --> 00:12:30,614
Investigators traced exactly how its rear rivets tore, and the damage only lines up geometrically if that flap was retracted — the setting for ordinary cruising flight, not for a landing.

119
00:12:31,733 --> 00:12:33,954
One ATSB investigator put the counter-argument plainly.

120
00:12:33,954 --> 00:12:44,742
If someone still had control and still had fuel, there was never a reason to run the tanks dry, then attempt an unpowered glide, when a powered landing had been available the whole time.

121
00:12:45,866 --> 00:12:50,812
And no large, intact piece of this aircraft has ever been recovered.

122
00:12:50,812 --> 00:13:00,704
Only small, fragmented pieces, consistent with a fast, high-energy impact — not a gentle water landing, which tends to leave much larger sections floating.

123
00:13:01,833 --> 00:13:07,367
Put all four of those together. The accelerating dive. The retracted flap. The fuel logic.

124
00:13:07,367 --> 00:13:08,474
The shattered debris.

125
00:13:08,474 --> 00:13:12,164
The official investigation's own physics all point the same direction.

126
00:13:12,164 --> 00:13:16,592
In its final minutes, nobody appears to have been flying this aircraft.

127
00:13:17,733 --> 00:13:19,372
It is not unanimous.

128
00:13:19,372 --> 00:13:26,337
Larry Vance, a career air-crash investigator, looked at the same flap photographs and reached the opposite conclusion.

129
00:13:26,337 --> 00:13:33,302
Extended, not retracted, meaning someone was flying it, carefully, all the way to a controlled water landing.

130
00:13:34,433 --> 00:13:39,869
ATSB officials responded to him directly, and publicly, standing by their own retracted-flap finding.

131
00:13:39,869 --> 00:13:51,517
When a career investigator and the agency that ran the actual search disagree this specifically, naming the disagreement honestly matters — but the official finding gets the last word here.

132
00:13:52,666 --> 00:14:04,584
A separate, peer-reviewed paper argues the standard math might be using the wrong model, that coupling the plane's heading changes with the signal, instead of treating the shift as purely vertical, is consistent with a gentler, more controlled ending.

133
00:14:04,584 --> 00:14:08,862
It's real science. It hasn't replaced the model the actual search was built on.

134
00:14:10,000 --> 00:14:12,501
One more small detail, easy to miss.

135
00:14:12,501 --> 00:14:27,868
At two twenty-five that morning, the satellite unit rebooted mid-flight, rare enough that some technical analysts read it as a specific interruption of one particular electrical bus — a detail that takes real, specific knowledge of the aircraft's own systems to even locate.

136
00:14:29,000 --> 00:14:31,586
Chapter seven. Where it stands now.

137
00:14:32,733 --> 00:14:37,944
While the documentary argued theories on a screen, a real ship kept searching a real ocean.

138
00:14:37,944 --> 00:14:48,041
Ocean Infinity's newest search ran in two phases, weeks in the spring of last year, then again from the very end of December into January, over five months at sea combined.

139
00:14:49,166 --> 00:14:52,353
It found nothing. Malaysia's seventy-million-dollar reward remains unpaid.

140
00:14:52,353 --> 00:15:00,320
The company's own chief executive says their commitment has not ended, and that they hope to return when circumstances allow.

141
00:15:01,466 --> 00:15:08,353
That specific search box traces back to a contested method — analyzing faint disturbances in worldwide amateur radio signals.

142
00:15:08,353 --> 00:15:11,977
It sits entirely outside the toolkit official investigators actually use.

143
00:15:11,977 --> 00:15:18,864
And yet it is the real reasoning that sent a real, government-funded ship to that exact patch of ocean.

144
00:15:20,000 --> 00:15:25,283
Less than half of that predicted area was actually covered before the ship had to leave.

145
00:15:25,283 --> 00:15:29,246
The hypothesis was never disproven there. It was simply never fully checked.

146
00:15:30,366 --> 00:15:36,479
This year, Malaysia's government approved another twelve months, running through next June, to finish covering what's left.

147
00:15:37,600 --> 00:15:46,926
Its transport minister called the extension, in his words, a manifestation of the government's continuous and unwavering commitment to provide closure for the families.

148
00:15:48,066 --> 00:15:49,978
There's one more real lead.

149
00:15:49,978 --> 00:15:59,534
Scientists re-examined recordings from underwater microphones built to detect nuclear tests, and found one faint signal, at one station, bearing roughly toward the search zone.

150
00:16:00,666 --> 00:16:06,431
It has not been picked up by a second station, and the exact bearing is disputed.

151
00:16:06,431 --> 00:16:11,115
Call it exactly what it is. A real, exciting lead. Not a location.

152
00:16:12,233 --> 00:16:13,641
Debris keeps surfacing too.

153
00:16:13,641 --> 00:16:23,500
Just last December, searcher Blaine Gibson found another piece on a beach in Madagascar, which he says supports a mid-air breakup, and argues against a slow, controlled landing.

154
00:16:23,500 --> 00:16:28,429
That is his own read of the evidence, not yet a confirmed official finding.

155
00:16:29,566 --> 00:16:37,358
And Jeff Wise, the same man behind two of this documentary's headline theories, published yet another new idea in February this year.

156
00:16:37,358 --> 00:16:44,795
This time, that the search's continued failure implies someone survived the dive and glided the aircraft further than anyone has searched.

157
00:16:45,933 --> 00:16:53,326
He named the captain specifically, while admitting, in his own words, it's hard to imagine why anyone would have wanted to do that.

158
00:16:54,466 --> 00:16:59,510
Three theories, from the same man, in three years, about the same eleven minutes of data.

159
00:16:59,510 --> 00:17:08,337
That alone should tell you something about how much of this case is still genuinely unsettled, and how little of the certainty in that documentary actually holds up.

160
00:17:09,466 --> 00:17:11,268
Chapter eight. The verdict.

161
00:17:12,400 --> 00:17:17,836
So the documentary's own harshest independent critic gets the final word on the show itself.

162
00:17:17,836 --> 00:17:29,798
In his assessment, it hands conspiracy, fabrication, and fantasy the same weight as hard evidence, and every one of its three headline theories is ruled out by the physical evidence that actually exists.

163
00:17:30,933 --> 00:17:37,281
And the official investigation gets the final word on the case itself, exactly as it did the day it was published.

164
00:17:38,400 --> 00:17:43,183
Unable to determine the real cause. Third-party interference not ruled out.

165
00:17:43,183 --> 00:17:45,793
No conclusive finding against any individual.

166
00:17:46,933 --> 00:17:53,922
Every theory in this video, and every theory in that documentary, sits on top of that same unresolved foundation.

167
00:17:53,922 --> 00:17:55,762
Nothing since has changed it.

168
00:17:56,900 --> 00:18:01,965
We didn't make this video to defend a documentary, or to tear one down for clicks.

169
00:18:01,965 --> 00:18:14,630
We made it because twelve years on, this mystery is still being sold as certainty, in half a dozen contradictory directions at once, and two hundred and thirty-nine families still don't have the one thing that would actually end it.

170
00:18:14,630 --> 00:18:15,264
The aircraft.

171
00:18:16,400 --> 00:18:21,389
Until someone finds it, every theory, ours included, is still just a theory.

172
00:18:22,533 --> 00:18:32,838
If this made you want the full, minute-by-minute story of how an investigation like this actually works, from the black box math to the ocean floor, we have another one on this channel.

173
00:18:32,838 --> 00:18:36,586
Air France four forty-seven, the flight we DID pull from the ocean.

174
00:18:36,586 --> 00:18:43,144
It took two years to find, and what its recorder actually captured is just as disturbing as anything in this documentary.

175
00:18:44,266 --> 00:18:50,464
If Air France four forty-seven's two-year search shocked you, wait until you see what its own black box actually recorded.

176
00:18:50,464 --> 00:18:52,013
Subscribe to Black Box Breakdown.

177
00:18:52,013 --> 00:19:00,070
We take the world's biggest aviation mysteries apart, piece by piece, and when the honest answer is that nobody knows yet, we tell you that too.

